  "transcript": "# Learning from Inquests\n\n## How inquests felt to families\n> Families described inquests as retraumatising, unequal and often powerless to trigger real change.\n\nChair: We asked witnesses with lived experience to explain why the coronial follow-up system needs to change.\nJohnny Santer: My son Gabriel died falling from a multi-storey car park in 2020. The inquest process was chaotic: I didn’t get the coroner’s bundle until the night before, and I had no lawyer then.\nRichard Caseby: My son Matthew died after escaping a private mental health unit and being struck by a train. I had to use my investigative background to uncover evidence and then rely on legal aid to get representation.\nDr Kate Szymankiewicz: Our daughter Ruth died in a privately run NHS-commissioned eating-disorder unit. The jury found unlawful killing, yet the coroner declined to issue a PFD and nothing meaningful changed.\nTessa Munt: Did you have realistic expectations about what an inquest could do before you started?\nJohnny Santer: I knew the basic questions a coroner asks, but not how to turn an inquest into prevention. Representation makes a huge difference: after I saw a barrister get a PFD in a similar case, I realised what could be done.\nRichard Caseby: I underestimated how adversarial inquests can be. Public bodies turn up with teams of lawyers funded by the state. Families are expected to be ‘at the heart’ but often are not.\nDr Kate Szymankiewicz: We only found INQUEST by chance; without that help we wouldn't have known to seek legal aid. The coroner initially seemed focused on narrow outcomes and not the systemic issues we wanted explored.\n\n\n## Prevention of Future Deaths reports: rare and limited\n> PFDs cover only a tiny share of inquests, coroners have wide discretion, and PFDs lack enforcement and consistent follow-up.\n\nVikki Slade: Only Richard’s case got a PFD. How useful was it, and why is enforcement a problem?\nRichard Caseby: The PFD highlighted fence height, record-keeping and risk-assessment failures. It helped: the Priory later raised fences and was prosecuted. But I could push because of my resources; many families can't.\nJohnny Santer: Gabriel’s death did not get a PFD. There are many virtually identical multi-storey car park deaths—Georgia’s tracker shows dozens—but no consistent action from operators like Q-Park.\nDr Kate Szymankiewicz: Even when juries find unlawful killing and list systemic failures, coroners sometimes decline PFDs after providers promise fixes; but there is often no checking that promises are kept.\nDr Georgia Richards: PFDs are issued in about 1.7% of inquests. Coroners decide whether to write one; courts confirmed that discretion in a 2022 legal case. That creates big geographic variation.\nDeborah Coles: PFDs can matter—there are successful campaigns such as Awaab’s law and Seni’s law—but many reports are formulaic, and organisations treat them as reputation-management rather than a safety duty.\nWarinder Juss: Would coroners be required to issue PFDs where culpability is found?\nDr Georgia Richards: No statutory threshold forces a PFD. The coroner’s discretion, uneven practice and lack of enforcement mean many important lessons are never captured or acted on.\n\n\n## Data and oversight gaps\n> There is no national database of all inquests; that prevents systematic analysis, trend-spotting and timely action.\n\nChair: What follow-up systems already exist and what is missing?\nDr Georgia Richards: There’s no single statutory database of all inquests. My Preventable Deaths Tracker collects every PFD since 2013 and tracks responses, but it’s privately funded and not a substitute for a national system.\nDr Georgia Richards: We found that responses improved from under a third to about two-thirds after the chief coroner started publishing non-response lists. For 2024 some reports show 92% with responses published—but publication rules and templates are now changing in ways that risk reversing progress.\nDeborah Coles: Without a national system, narrative conclusions—where juries describe systemic failures—are lost unless highlighted by charities or families. There is no routine follow-up to check whether recommended changes happen on the ground.\nTony Vaughan: What models exist abroad?\nDr Georgia Richards: Australia, Canada and others have national coronial databases. Australia’s system grew after a royal commission into deaths in custody. These systems enable trend analysis and policy response; we could adopt a similar statutory database.\nMrs Russell: Is your tracker public?\nDr Georgia Richards: Yes, but it has limited funding, and some content sits behind a paywall because there was no Government funding after the Committee’s 2024 evidence session. We need a publicly funded digital system.\nDeborah Coles: A national oversight mechanism (NOM) would need the database to feed it: collect recommendations and inquest findings, analyse themes, and crucially follow up with powers to require information and work with regulators.\n\n\n## Practical reforms families and experts want\n> Proposals include a national database, an independent oversight body to follow up recommendations, early legal funding, digitalisation and clearer coroner guidance.\n\nChair: If you could give Government one message about improving learning from inquests, what would it be?\nDr Kate Szymankiewicz: Keep the human cost front and centre: create an oversight mechanism that actually makes change happen and brings Ministers from relevant Departments together.\nJohnny Santer: Families just want lessons learned so others don’t die the same way. If private companies face no real consequence they won’t act—there needs to be power and accountability.\nRichard Caseby: End the cliff-edge after PFDs: appoint a commissioner for prevention of future deaths with a small secretariat to spot recurring failings and hold organisations to account.\nDeborah Coles: The NOM should be independent, able to request information, follow up implementation, and report to Parliament. Families must be part of its governance so lived experience shapes priorities.\nDr Georgia Richards: Digitalise and publish in real time: a statutory national database of all inquests is the foundation. With live dashboards you can spot trends quickly and evaluate policies such as Hillsborough Law.\nTessa Munt: What about legal representation?\nDeborah Coles: Early access to legal aid saves families retraumatisation and improves scrutiny. The trigger point for funding should be earlier than now so families get advice before pre-inquest hearings.\nDr Georgia Richards: And evaluate policies: a national database lets you measure whether changes actually reduce similar deaths rather than just producing a report that gathers dust.\nChair: That concludes the session. Thank you all for your courage and for your practical proposals.\nDeborah Coles: Please act. Families have campaigned for decades; an independent mechanism would finally make learning from deaths consistent, transparent and accountable.\nDr Georgia Richards: We already have tools and international examples. With modest investment we can stop this being a postcode lottery and save lives.\n",
  "inquiry": "Learning from Inquests",
